<%--
Api_Confirm_Dialog - the console's own yes/no dialog.

FRAMEWORK-OWNED ON PURPOSE. The console runs inside an application's page, but it may not
borrow that application's dialog system: an app is free to restyle Modal, replace it, or not
ship one at all, and a framework feature that reached for it would break on exactly the
install that customised the most. So the console asks its own question, in its own markup,
painted from its own --api-* custom properties.

It is a CONFIRM and nothing else - two buttons, one boolean. It is not a modal system, and it
must not grow into one; a second kind of question belongs to whichever feature needs it.
--%>
<Define:Api_Confirm_Dialog tag="div">
<div $sid="backdrop" class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__backdrop"></div>
<div $sid="panel" class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__panel" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="<%= this.args.title %>">
    <div class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__title"><%= this.args.title %></div>
    <div class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__body"><%= this.args.body %></div>
    <div class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__actions">
        <button type="button" $sid="cancel" class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__btn"><%= this.args.cancel_label %></button>
        <button type="button" $sid="confirm" class="Api_Confirm_Dialog__btn Api_Confirm_Dialog__btn--confirm"><%= this.args.confirm_label %></button>
    </div>
</div>
</Define:Api_Confirm_Dialog>
